Que: You're getting a timeout error from an API — walk me through your full debugging approach.
First, identify scope: is it one endpoint or all endpoints, one instance or all? Check API Gateway/load balancer logs for the exact 
timeout point (client-side, gateway, or backend). Check the target service's health and thread pool status — is it stuck processing, 
or not responding at all? Look at downstream dependencies (DB, external APIs) the endpoint calls — a slow DB query or a hung external call is the most common cause.
Check connection pool exhaustion (DB/HTTP client pools maxed out). Verify network issues (DNS, firewall, latency between services). Check recent deployments/config changes as a
likely trigger. If it's a distributed call, use trace IDs (Zipkin/Jaeger) to pinpoint exactly which hop is timing out. Once found, apply a quick fix (restart, scale, kill stuck thread) 
to restore service, then root-cause it properly afterward.

Que: 2. Your microservice is slow under high load — how do you find the bottleneck?
Start with metrics: check CPU, memory, thread pool usage, and GC activity — resource saturation is the most common cause. Check DB query performance and connection pool size — 
slow queries or pool exhaustion under load is a frequent culprit. Look at downstream/external API calls — a slow dependency without a timeout can back up the entire request chain. 
Check thread dumps for blocked/waiting threads to spot lock contention or synchronous bottlenecks. Review caching — missing or expired caches under high load increase DB pressure. 
Check if the service is stateless and horizontally scalable, or if a single instance is a bottleneck. Use APM tools (New Relic, Dynatrace) for code-level profiling to find the 
slowest method/query. Load test with the same traffic pattern in staging to reproduce and confirm the fix before deploying to production.

Que: 3. Production bug, no monitoring tools allowed, logs only — but multiple concurrent users are hitting at the same time. How do you isolate the issue?
Without APM, rely entirely on structured, correlated logs. First, ensure each request has a unique correlation/trace ID propagated across all log statements and services — this is
essential to separate one user's request flow from another's in concurrent traffic. Grep logs by that trace ID to reconstruct the exact sequence of events for a specific failing 
request. Filter by timestamp range around when the bug was reported to narrow the window. Look for stack traces, error codes, or unusual patterns (repeated retries, timeouts) near 
the failure point. Check log levels — temporarily bump to DEBUG for the affected service if possible, without restarting others. Cross-reference logs across services in the request 
chain (API Gateway → Service A → Service B) to find where the flow breaks. This log-only approach depends heavily on having consistent, structured logging (JSON with trace IDs) 
already in place before the incident.
